      "content": "That makes sense. Using those markers as proxies for \"social capital\" seems more objective than just guessing. So, if we were to look at real-world data to weight these, how would we handle a situation where different demographics have different baseline levels for these markers? For example, if we're comparing two patients but one comes from an area with historically higher unemployment and lower education averages, while the other comes from a highly stable, affluent area, how would your framework suggest we weight their \"recovery potential\" without being purely punitive toward the less stable individual?",
